export const SECURITY_POLICY_MD = "# Security Policy\n\n**Version:** 3.1 · **Effective:** 2026-11-14 · **Owner:** security@paciolus.com · **Classification:** Public\n\nVersion 3.1 (2026-11-14) adds the database backups that went live that day and narrows four sentences\nto what the code shows (CSRF scope, query scoping, upload limits, transport). It replaces version 2.6, which described an organization Paciolus is not (a CISO, an on-call\nrotation, annual penetration tests, monthly encryption verification, a database \"architecturally\nimpossible\" to breach). This version describes what is actually in place, so a customer can rely on\nevery sentence. The previous text is kept for the record in\n[`archive/SECURITY_POLICY_pre-sprint-845_2026-10-24.md`](archive/SECURITY_POLICY_pre-sprint-845_2026-10-24.md).\n\n## 1. Who runs Paciolus\n\nPaciolus is built and operated by its founder. There is no security team and no 24/7 on-call.\nSecurity issues are handled by the founder as soon as they are seen. Report one to\n**security@paciolus.com** (see the [Vulnerability Disclosure Policy](VULNERABILITY_DISCLOSURE_POLICY.md)).\n\n## 2. Certifications and testing\n\n- **No SOC 2 audit has been performed and none is currently scheduled.**\n- **No third-party penetration test has been performed.** There is no bug bounty.\n- No ISO 27001, HIPAA or similar certification is held or claimed.\n\n## 3. What data exists and where\n\n| Data | Where | Notes |\n|------|-------|-------|\n| Uploaded files | Server memory, for the request | Not saved to the database or file storage. A previewed PDF or workbook is held in memory for up to 10 minutes so the tool can reuse it. |\n| Accounts, client metadata, settings | Neon PostgreSQL (US East, us-east-1) | |\n| Analysis summaries | Neon | Category totals, ratios, row counts, total debits/credits, materiality, flagged account names (no amounts). |\n| Engagement entries | Neon | What the user types: materiality, follow-ups, intercompany mappings, analytical expectations, uncorrected misstatements (with amounts). |\n| Shared report files | Cloudflare R2 (`paciolus-exports`) | Only reports a user chooses to share by link; 24 h (Professional) or 48 h (Enterprise), then deleted. |\n| Rate-limit counters | Render Key Value | Request counts keyed by user or IP. |\n| Database backups | Cloudflare R2 (`paciolus-backups`) | A daily `pg_dump` of the Neon database, made by a GitHub Actions job with a read-only role; 30 daily and 12 monthly copies. |\n| Application logs | Render log stream (and Grafana Cloud Loki if enabled) | No uploaded file contents; exception type names only; tracebacks stripped in production. |\n| Error reports | Sentry | No request bodies, frame local variables, exception messages or breadcrumbs (stripped before sending). |\n| Payments | Stripe | Card data never reaches Paciolus. |\n| Email | SendGrid | Verification, password reset and billing notices. |\n\n## 4. Controls in place\n\n**Transport.** HTTPS (TLS) is provided by our hosts, Vercel (frontend) and Render (backend); the TLS\nversions offered are theirs. HSTS, a nonce-based Content-Security-Policy with `strict-dynamic`, `frame-ancestors 'none'`\nand `no-store` on downloads. Strict CORS allowlist.\n\n**At rest.** Encryption at rest is provided and managed by Neon and Cloudflare R2. Paciolus does not\nrun its own encryption-at-rest layer and does not separately verify the providers' encryption.\n\n**Authentication.** Passwords hashed with bcrypt (12 rounds). Access tokens expire after 15 minutes and are held in memory\nin the browser; refresh tokens are HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies rotated on use with reuse detection\n(reuse revokes every session). A password change revokes all sessions. Five failed logins lock an\naccount for 15 minutes. Unknown-email and locked-account logins take the same time as real ones.\n**There is no multi-factor authentication in the app**, including for the platform administrator.\n\n**Request integrity.** State-changing requests made while signed in carry CSRF tokens HMAC-bound to the\nuser, plus Origin and Fetch-Metadata checks. Pre-sign-in routes (login, registration, password reset),\nthe Stripe webhook, the contact form and the waitlist are exempt.\nEvery route is rate-limited (60 requests/minute by default, tighter on authentication, upload,\nexport and write endpoints).\n\n**Tenancy.** Customer-data queries are scoped to the signed-in account in application code (the\nplatform-administrator routes in §4 are deliberately not). There is\nno database row-level security. This is designed to prevent one customer reading another's data;\nno isolation guarantee beyond that is made.\n\n**Uploads.** Size (100 MB), row (500,000), column (1,000) and cell-length (100,000 characters) limits; file-signature checks; archive-bomb\nlimits (uncompressed size and ratio, per format); `defusedxml` for XML; a 500-page cap on PDFs.\n\n**Exports.** Every CSV cell is escaped against spreadsheet formula injection by one shared writer.\nShared-report passcodes (optional) are hashed with Argon2id and throttled per token and per client IP.\n\n**Administrator access.** One platform administrator role (superadmin). It can view customer accounts\nand start a **read-only, 15-minute impersonation session**, which is recorded in the admin audit log and\ncannot write. Revenue metrics are visible only to that role.\n\n## 5. Monitoring and incidents\n\nErrors go to Sentry. A GitHub Actions probe checks the live site every 15 minutes (GitHub may delay\nscheduled runs). Security events — failed logins, lockouts, refresh-token reuse, impersonation,\naccount erasure — are written to the logs and, for the critical ones, to a `security_events` table\nkept for 365 days. See the [Incident Response Plan](INCIDENT_RESPONSE_PLAN.md).\n\n## 6. Backups and recovery\n\nThe database is copied every day with `pg_dump` to Cloudflare R2, a separate provider, by a GitHub\nActions job that uses a read-only database role. A weekly check confirms the newest backup is complete\nand recent, and a monthly test restores a backup into a temporary database and compares it. Neon's\npoint-in-time recovery is available in addition. The first full restore test passed on 2026-11-14.\nSee [Business Continuity](BUSINESS_CONTINUITY_DISASTER_RECOVERY.md).\n\n## 7. Changes\n\nCode changes go through pull requests with automated tests, linting and security scanning (see\n[Secure SDL](SECURE_SDL_CHANGE_MANAGEMENT.md)). This policy is updated when the facts above change.\n"

export const SUBPROCESSOR_LIST_MD = "# Subprocessor List\n\n**Version:** 2.1 · **Last updated:** 2026-11-14 · **Owner:** privacy@paciolus.com · **Classification:** Public\n\nThe third parties that process personal data for Paciolus. Version 1.1 listed the database as\n\"Render (Oregon)\" (it is Neon, US East), omitted Neon and Render Key Value, marked every provider's DPA\n\"Signed\" without a record of signature, and described share links as passcode-protected (the passcode is\noptional). The previous text is in\n[`archive/SUBPROCESSOR_LIST_pre-sprint-845_2026-10-24.md`](archive/SUBPROCESSOR_LIST_pre-sprint-845_2026-10-24.md).\n\nWe will update this list before adding a subprocessor and will tell customers who ask to be notified\n(privacy@paciolus.com).\n\n## Active Subprocessors\n\n| Provider | What it does | Personal data it processes | Region |\n|----------|-------------|----------------------------|--------|\n| **Neon** | PostgreSQL database | Accounts (email, name, password hash), client metadata, analysis summaries, flagged account names, engagement entries, activity history, security events | United States (us-east-1) |\n| **Render** | Backend hosting and application logs | API requests in transit; logs (no uploaded file contents) | United States |\n| **Render Key Value** | Rate limiting | Request counters keyed by user ID or IP address | United States |\n| **Vercel** | Frontend hosting and CDN | IP address, browser details, access logs | United States / global edge |\n| **Cloudflare** | R2 object storage for shared reports (`paciolus-exports`) and database backups (`paciolus-backups`) | The PDF / Excel / CSV file a user shares by link, for 24 hours (Professional) or 48 hours (Enterprise); keys are hashes of the share token. A daily copy of the whole database, kept as 30 daily and 12 monthly copies | Eastern North America |\n| **GitHub** | Runs the daily database backup and the restore tests (GitHub Actions) | The whole database, in transit, while a backup or restore test runs on a GitHub-hosted runner | Chosen by GitHub |\n| **Stripe** | Payments and subscriptions | Email, billing details, payment method (card data never reaches Paciolus) | United States |\n| **SendGrid** | Transactional email | Email address, name, message content (verification, password reset, billing notices) | United States |\n| **Sentry** | Error monitoring | User ID, error type and stack location; request bodies, local variables, exception messages and breadcrumbs are stripped before sending | United States |\n\n**Grafana Cloud (Loki)** receives a copy of the application logs only if log shipping is enabled in\nproduction; it is listed here when it is.\n\nEach provider's standard data processing terms apply. Which terms have been formally accepted, and the\ntransfer mechanism each uses, is being confirmed and will be recorded here.\n\n## Not subprocessors\n\nGitHub's source-code hosting, Cloudflare DNS and Dependabot handle no customer data. (GitHub Actions does, for backups — listed above.)\n\n## Change log\n\n| Date | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| 2026-11-14 | v2.1 — database backups added: Cloudflare R2 `paciolus-backups` and GitHub Actions (which runs the backup and restore tests) listed; share-link retention stated per plan. |\n| 2026-10-24 | v2.0 — rewritten to the facts (Sprint 845): Neon and Render Key Value added, regions corrected, unverified \"DPA Signed\" and certification columns removed. |\n| 2026-10-23 | v1.1 — Cloudflare R2 added; Google Fonts eliminated (fonts self-hosted). |\n"

export const DATA_HANDLING_ARCHITECTURE_MD = "# Data Handling Architecture\n\n**Version:** 3.1 · **Effective:** 2026-11-14 · **Owner:** security@paciolus.com · **Classification:** Public\n\n*(v3.1, 2026-11-14: renamed from its earlier filename, database backups added, and the deletion section\ncorrected for them. Earlier versions are in [`archive/`](archive/).)*\n\n## In one sentence\n\nYour uploaded files are processed in memory and not kept after analysis; we save your account, client\nlist, analysis summaries and anything you enter in an engagement.\n\n## The life of an upload\n\n1. **Receive.** The file arrives in the request body (capped at 110 MB; files at 100 MB / 500,000\n   rows). Multipart parts are kept in memory up to the request cap rather than spooled to disk.\n2. **Validate.** Signature, size, row/column/cell-length limits, archive-bomb limits per format, `defusedxml`.\n3. **Analyse** in memory. If you previewed the file first, its bytes are held in an in-memory cache for\n   up to 10 minutes, bound to your account and consumable once.\n4. **Respond** with the results.\n5. **Release.** The file and its rows leave memory. A **summary** is saved to your history: category\n   totals, ratios, row counts, total debits and credits, materiality, anomaly counts, the first\n   characters of the filename and its hash — and, when a tool flags accounts, their **names** (no amounts).\n\nNothing from the upload is saved to the database or to file storage, and nothing from it reaches the logs or Sentry: application\nlogs carry exception type names only, and Sentry events have request bodies, local variables, exception\nmessages and breadcrumbs removed before they are sent.\n\n## 2. What is stored, and where\n\n### 2.1 Summary\n\n| Data | Store | Retention |\n|------|-------|-----------|\n| **Accounts and settings** | Neon PostgreSQL, us-east-1 | Until account deletion |\n| **Client metadata** | Neon | Until deleted |\n| **Analysis summaries** | Neon | 365 days, then archived; deleted with the account |\n| **Tool Run Records** | Neon | Run status, score and the flagged account names (no amounts) for each tool run in an engagement |\n| **Engagement entries** (materiality, follow-ups, intercompany mappings, analytical expectations, uncorrected misstatements) | Neon | Until you delete them or the account |\n| **Sessions and security events** | Neon | Session tokens hashed, until expiry; security events (hashed IP) 365 days |\n| **Shared report files** | Cloudflare R2 `paciolus-exports`, keyed by a hash of the share token | 24 h (Professional) / 48 h (Enterprise); the DB row is removed only after R2 confirms deletion |\n| **Rate-limit counters** | Render Key Value | Short-lived |\n| **Database backups** — a daily copy of everything in Neon above | Cloudflare R2 `paciolus-backups` (a separate provider), written by a GitHub Actions job using a read-only database role | 30 daily and 12 monthly copies. A deleted account stays in older backups until they age out |\n\nExported PDFs, Excel and CSV files are generated on request and sent to the browser without being\nsaved — except a report you share by link, as above.\n\n### 2.2 Controlled Storage Exceptions\n\nEvery table that holds amounts or account identifiers, so nothing is stored that this page does not\nname. (`backend/tests/test_zero_storage_disclosure.py` fails the build if a table with money or account\ncolumns is missing here.)\n\n#### Users Table\n\nEmail, name, bcrypt password hash, tier, settings. Until account deletion.\n\n#### Clients Table\n\nClient name, industry, fiscal year-end. Until deleted.\n\n#### Activity Logs Table\n\nPer analysis: row count, total debits and credits, materiality, balanced flag, anomaly counts, first characters of the filename and its hash. Archived after 365 days.\n\n#### Diagnostic Summaries Table\n\nPer analysis: category totals (assets, liabilities, revenue, expenses …), ratios, total debits and credits. Archived after 365 days.\n\n#### Tool Sessions Table\n\nSaved adjusting entries (accounts and amounts) and currency rate tables, for 1–2 hours.\n\n#### Engagements Table\n\nPeriod, status, materiality amounts the user sets. Until deleted.\n\n#### Tool Runs Table\n\nPer tool run in an engagement: status, composite score and the names of flagged accounts (no amounts).\n\n#### Follow-Up Items Table\n\nNarratives the user writes about flagged items, and comments.\n\n#### Subscriptions Table\n\nPlan, status, seats, Stripe IDs, upload counts.\n\n#### Billing Events Table\n\nPlan changes and payments (tier, interval, amounts in metadata); kept with the user unlinked after erasure.\n\n#### Uncorrected Misstatements Table\n\nWhat the user enters: accounts affected with debit/credit and amount, F/S impact.\n\n#### Analytical Expectations Table\n\nWhat the user enters: expected values, precision thresholds, actual values and variances.\n\n#### Admin Audit Logs Table\n\nSuperadmin actions, including credit and refund amounts. Append-only.\n\n#### Tool Activities Table\n\nPer tool use: tool name, first characters of the filename, and a short caller-supplied summary (up to 2,000 characters).\n\n### 2.3 What is not kept\n\nUploaded files, and the line-level rows in them — individual balances, journal entries, invoices,\npayments and payroll records — except where those rows are inside a report a user shares by link\n(§2.1).\n\n## Deletion\n\n\"Clear history\" and \"delete engagement\" **archive** records. **Deleting your account**\n(Settings → Profile → Your Data) physically removes everything above that belongs to you from the\ndatabase, cancels your subscription with Stripe and removes your shared files from R2; billing history is\nkept with your identity removed. You can download everything first from the same page.\n\n**Backups.** Copies of the database made before the deletion still contain your data until those backups\nage out under the retention in §2.1 (up to 12 months for the oldest monthly copy). Backups are used only\nto restore the service and to test, once a month, that a restore works.\n\n## Why it matters, accurately stated\n\nNot keeping uploads means uploaded files are not at rest to leak — except the rows inside a report a user\nshares by link, for its 24 or 48 hours. It does **not** mean there is nothing to protect: the summaries, engagement entries and backups above are client financial\ncontent and are protected as described in the [Security Policy](SECURITY_POLICY.md).\n"

